# deepseek-flash without thinking

This applies to api.deepseek.com.

What the model still does when `thinking` is disabled, measured against the live
API on 2026-10-01. Model id `deepseek-flash`, i.e. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, served by
DeepSeek's own endpoint. Readings come from `research/flash_probe.py`; every
number below was produced by a call made while writing this, not quoted from a
benchmark.

The short version: disabling thinking removes the chain of thought and nothing
else. Tools, images, `stop`, JSON mode and FIM all still run. What it costs is
instruction discipline — the answers stay right, the surface form drifts.

## The switch

Thinking is on by default at effort `high`. The toggle sits at the top level of
the request body next to `model`, not inside `messages`.

```json
{"model": "deepseek-flash", "messages": [...], "thinking": {"type": "disabled"}}
```

`reasoning_effort` (`low` | `high` | `max`) is the effort dial and is meaningful
only while thinking is on. In the Anthropic-shaped API the same choice is
`{"reasoning": {"effort": "none"}}` on the `/anthropic` base URL; `none` is the
off switch there. In this repo `deepseek.chat.Request.thinking` is the field;
`chat.Thinking.disabled` is the value the playground sends.

With thinking off the reply carries no `reasoning_content` key at all — not an
empty string — and `usage.completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` is null.
`top_p` is documented as pinned to 1.0 in this mode, so a `top_p` value is
accepted and ignored; `temperature`, `presence_penalty` and `frequency_penalty`
are documented as ignored in thinking mode and therefore expected to apply here.
That expectation is the guide's, not a measurement: nothing in this note tests
the effect of sampling parameters.

## Still works with thinking off

One call per cell, `--mode off`:

| Case | Request | Observed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Arithmetic | `17*19`, number only | `323` |
| Multi-step | bat and ball, in cents | `5` (correct trap answer) |
| Exact output | reply with exactly `OK` | `OK` |
| JSON object | `response_format: json_object` | `{"city": "Lisbon", "population": 545000}` |
| Tool call, forced | `tool_choice: "required"` | one `get_weather` call, `finish_reason: tool_calls` |
| Tool call, auto | tools offered, greeting asked | text answer, no call |
| Stop sequences | `stop: ["3"]`, count upward | `1\n2\n`, `finish_reason: stop` |
| Vision | 64×64 solid-red PNG as data URI | `Red` |
| FIM | `/beta/completions`, prefix + suffix | valid function body |

The forced-tool row is the one that only exists in this mode. Thinking mode
answers the same request with 400 `Thinking mode does not support this
tool_choice`. That is why `thinking: disabled` is the setting for any agent loop
that dispatches a specific tool rather than hoping the model picks one.

The FIM row is not mode-gated in practice — see *Where the deployment disagrees
with the published docs*.

## What the chain of thought costs

`--suite` runs eight checkable tasks at three settings, one sample each. `off`
means `{"type": "disabled"}`; `high` and `max` are thinking with that effort.

| Task | off | high | max |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 9.11 vs 9.8 | ok, 3 tok | ok, 68 tok | ok, 49 tok |
| r's in strawberry | miss (prose), 54 tok | ok, 141 tok | ok, 53 tok |
| year arithmetic | ok, 2 tok | ok, 90 tok | ok, 144 tok |
| digit reverse | miss (`8147 - 7418 = 729`), 9 tok | ok, 58 tok | ok, 61 tok |
| weekday of 2026-10-01 | ok, 1 tok | ok, 310 tok | ok, 355 tok |
| email extraction | ok, 12 tok | ok, 286 tok | ok, 118 tok |
| one-line bug fix, executed | ok, 5 tok | ok, 82 tok | ok, 142 tok |
| 2^100 mod 1000 | ok, 1 tok | ok, 188 tok | ok, 217 tok |

Totals: 6/8 strict hits with thinking off, 8/8 with thinking on. Both off-mode
misses are format, not substance. The digit-reverse reply states the right number
inside a written sentence; the strawberry reply explains the count instead of
returning it, and re-running that prompt with thinking off three times returned a
bare `3` every time. This is variance at n=1, and the honest reading is "the off
mode is less reliable at obeying a shape constraint", not "the off mode cannot
count".

Nearly all of the difference is reasoning tokens. `weekday` costs 1 output token
without thinking and 310 with it, 308 of those being reasoning. Nothing but the
effort setting differs between the columns.

The same effect shows up as latency at the top end: a one-line JSON request for
Lisbon's population took 1,268 reasoning tokens and 7.9 s at `high`, against 14
output tokens and 0.8 s with thinking off.

## Where the deployment disagrees with the published docs

Two documented rules do not hold on the endpoint this repo talks to. Each was
checked with a request built by hand rather than through the probe script; the
first was sent twice, the second in two shapes.

1. The thinking-mode guide says a request that disables thinking while setting
   `reasoning_effort` is refused. It is not:

   ```json
   {"model": "deepseek-flash", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "hi"}],
    "thinking": {"type": "disabled"}, "reasoning_effort": "high"}
   ```

   answers 200 with no reasoning content. The published 400 is therefore not
   something `Request` needs to prevent, and a caller passing both is not
   currently punished for it. Do not rely on the reverse either: the response is
   ordinary non-thinking output, so the effort value is simply dropped, and
   whether that stays true is DeepSeek's to change.

2. The pricing table marks FIM completion "Non-thinking mode only". FIM returns
   content with `thinking: {"type": "enabled"}` and with the `thinking` field
   omitted entirely (the default is thinking). Nothing in the reply distinguishes
   the two.

   The library should keep sending `thinking: disabled` on the FIM path anyway:
   the documented restriction may be enforced later, or on another provider, and
   nothing is lost by complying.

## Tool replay

When a request carries `tools`, thinking mode requires each previous assistant
turn's `reasoning_content` to come back, and `AssistantMessage` in
`src/remote/deepseek.zig` writes it whenever it is not null so that an empty
chain of thought is still transmitted. That rule is about thinking mode.

With thinking off, replaying an assistant tool call with the field absent
entirely is accepted, and so is replaying it as `""`. Both were confirmed
against the live API in a two-turn `get_weather` exchange. The library's current
behaviour — write it when non-null — is therefore correct in both modes and
needs no branch.

## Re-running

```sh
python3 research/flash_probe.py --mode off
python3 research/flash_probe.py --mode high
python3 research/flash_probe.py --suite
```

The key is read the way the library reads it: `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` first, then
omp's store through the installed `omp-keys.py` helper. `--suite` is eight
tasks at three settings; `--mode` is the full case list at one setting. The
script asserts nothing and keeps no fixtures.

## Sources

Published material, read on 2026-10-01. Where a claim above rests on one of
these rather than on a call, the text says so.

https://api-docs.deepseek.com/guides/thinking_mode/
https://api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing/
https://api-docs.deepseek.com/updates/
https://api-docs.deepseek.com/guides/fim_completion/
https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash

Two community sources worth reading alongside this note. Artificial Analysis
measures the model's output volume on its own suite and flags it as verbose with
thinking on. The OpenCode configuration splits the Flash tier by mode instead of
by model id, sending its cheapest agents — exploration, lookup, UI work — to
Flash with thinking off and `temperature: 0`.

https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/deepseek-v4-1-flash
https://github.com/znlgis/my-opencode-deepseek-config
